1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:07,282
This G one repeats a turning motion under manual pushes and zero-to-sixty-millisecond added delay.

2
00:00:10,250 --> 00:00:14,426
Randomization must balance exposure against learnability.

3
00:00:14,666 --> 00:00:19,874
Joint error mixes execution mismatch with torque-generating offsets.

4
00:00:20,114 --> 00:00:25,562
LUCID compares command and execution histories in a learned temporal space.

5
00:00:27,250 --> 00:00:31,738
Issued commands pass through simulated delay and dynamics.

6
00:00:31,978 --> 00:00:36,490
Command and execution histories enter the same frozen encoder.

7
00:00:36,730 --> 00:00:40,930
Their normalized latent discrepancy guides the next training block.

8
00:00:43,250 --> 00:00:50,498
First, a denoising temporal V A E learns to reconstruct clean motion from noisy windows.

9
00:00:50,738 --> 00:00:56,018
We freeze its encoder, keeping the comparison fixed as the policy learns.

10
00:00:57,250 --> 00:01:03,010
Bounded P I adjusts intensity using the learned gap and accumulated error.

11
00:01:03,250 --> 00:01:06,562
Two low-return blocks trigger a separate backoff.

12
00:01:06,802 --> 00:01:11,242
One intensity sets all six training ranges for the next block.

13
00:01:16,250 --> 00:01:20,018
Training uses up to forty milliseconds of added delay.

14
00:01:20,258 --> 00:01:28,178
Frozen policies face sixty milliseconds on full held-out motions, fifty percent above the training maximum.

15
00:01:30,250 --> 00:01:40,522
Under unseen sixty-millisecond delay, completion rises from fifty-two point one percent with filtered-error P I to seventy-three point eight with LUCID.

16
00:01:40,762 --> 00:01:44,194
That is a twenty-one point seven percentage-point gain.

17
00:01:48,250 --> 00:01:52,354
Denoising raises completion over ordinary reconstruction.

18
00:01:52,594 --> 00:01:57,058
Live feedback also improves on one donor’s replayed schedule.

19
00:02:00,250 --> 00:02:06,490
These selected demonstrations combine forty milliseconds of added delay with scheduled pushes.

20
00:02:06,730 --> 00:02:11,146
LUCID is on the left; filtered-error P I is on the right.

21
00:02:11,386 --> 00:02:17,962
They show behavior under perturbation. Aggregate completion comes from separate benchmark trials.

22
00:02:24,250 --> 00:02:28,594
The same hardware recording has continued throughout this presentation.

23
00:02:28,834 --> 00:02:38,626
Separately, the paper’s matched forty-millisecond test reports thirty-eight of sixty completions for LUCID, versus twenty-three for filtered-error P I.

24
00:02:38,866 --> 00:02:42,658
Only the policy and low-level controller run onboard.

25
00:02:48,250 --> 00:02:54,082
Learn robustness during training. Deploy only the policy and low-level controller.
